A few general categories of security event data, and how they can be used in an investigation

1.NETWORK INFORMATION
Network packets can be incredibly valuable in an investigation. 
NetFlow and and other Flow logs are examples of tools that capture network metadata, based on a sampling of network packets. In this type of capture, only headers and general identifying information is collected. 
On the other hand, you can capture actual copies of the 1s and 0s that travel through the network (wire data), and store it in a Packet Capture (PCAP) file format. This type of data can be very large in volume, which means it must be captured and stored with discretion.

2.AUTHENTICATION INFORMATION
Information about your assets, like servers or other resources, or identity information related to users or roles are essential when investigating security events.
This category includes events from a system like Active Directory, LDAP, RADIUS, TACACS+, Single Sign-On solutions like Okta or Azure AD, AWS Cloudtrail logs, VPN logs, and authentication specific logs from any other source.

3.PROXY AND GATEWAY INFORMATION
Web proxies or web gateways sit in the middle of communications between users and websites. They can provide useful information to analysts about which websites have been visited by which users.
Depending on a web proxy's configuration, it may even be inspecting secure HTTPS connections, allowing analysts to identify potential "hidden" traffic or accidental downloads of malicious files. 
Similarly, email gateways manage and secure email traffic, and can be a source of information about emails from suspicious or known malicious domains as well as any transferred files or included URLs. 

4.ANTIVIRUS OR ANTIMALWARE LOGS
Anti-virus and anti-malware logs are extremely important when investigating infections or outbreaks within an organization. 
The signatures for malicious software may also be helpful when researching more about threats that have been detected in the environment. 

Network Traffic
data is captured bit by bit, it is called a packet capture, or "pcaps." This is a full copy of the traffic that traveled over a network.
Packet captures, while helpful, contain a LOT of data! So, some other common means for recording or analyzing network traffic include the following:

1. NetFlow: for per device implementation (read more about the protocol at RFC 3954(opens in a new tab))
2. VPC Flow Logs: for cloud-based capture in AWS or Google Cloud environments
3. NSG Flow Logs: a feature of Azure Network Watcher for use in Azure cloud environments

Analyzing network traffic in PCAP format is something that is generally done AFTER an event. So what about real-time monitoring?

If the payload of network traffic is inspected in real-time, it's called Deep Packet Inspection (DPI). This is something that can be done by some security solutions, however given the data volume, real-time Deep Packet Inspection is often not feasible. 

Intrusion Detection Systems and Intrusion Prevention Systems (IDS/IPS)
Intrusion Detection Systems and Intrusion Prevention Systems are types of devices that monitor network traffic for known suspicious activity based on signatures. 
Whether detecting and alerting to an event, or detecting and blocking activity, these systems can be instrumental when attempting to stitch together various parts of a given attack. Many systems can provide both IDS and IPS capabilities, based on their implementation.

A few examples of network-based systems include Snort, Suricata, Cisco Firepower Next-Generation IPS, and Palo Alto Networks.
A few examples of host-based systems include McAfee, OSSEC, and Tripwire.

